  "preamble": "The voiceless alveolar sibilant /s/ becomes the voiced dental fricative /ð/, losing its sibilant groove, shifting forward to a dental place and gaining voicing. In Fijian, Proto-Oceanic *s regularly became c [ð], as in *masakit 'sick' > macake. In Tezoatlán Mixtec (part of the Silacayoapan Mixtec complex), Proto-Mixtec *s is written ⟨d⟩ = [ð], where most other Mixtec varieties keep s, as in *kɨ̀sɨ́ 'clay pot' > ki̱di. The change is found only in a few unrelated languages.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Voicing, Dentalization, Desibilantization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Fijian",
      "languageFamily": "Central Pacific",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Oceanic *masakit 'sick, painful'",
          "to": "macake",
          "note": "Fijian ⟨c⟩ = [ð]."
        },
